EXT. BORDER CHECKPOINT - DAWN

Mist on a concrete lane. A barrier arm, striped red and white. A kiosk with one lit window.

Grieve, 50s, border officer, tired, holds a clipboard against the cold. Opposite him stands Okonkwo, 40s, a solicitor in a sodden trenchcoat, a briefcase at her feet. Behind her in the car, Tomasz, 30s, and a small girl, Lena, 6, asleep on his shoulder.

INSERT - THE PASSPORT. The photograph does not match the face in the car.

Silence.

Hobbs, 30s, a sergeant, appears in the kiosk window with a phone to his ear.

OKONKWO
I've been very patient, Officer Grieve. I've been patient since four this morning. I would like to remind you that my client has a hearing at ten in Rotterdam, that the girl has a right to be with her father, and that the paperwork you are holding is, quite frankly, immaculate.

GRIEVE
It is immaculate. That's what bothers me.

OKONKWO
Is that a legal objection?

GRIEVE
It's an observation.

OKONKWO
Then you'll understand if I write it down.

GRIEVE
Please do. Spell it with a "k."

OKONKWO
(beat)
That's not funny.

GRIEVE
It wasn't meant to be.

HOBBS
(on the phone, from the kiosk)
Sir, I've got the Home Office on the line. They say the order's been revoked.

Grieve doesn't turn.

GRIEVE
Revoked when?

VANE (V.O.)
(on the phone, tinny, crisp)
Eleven fifty-two last night, Mr Grieve. Mr Vane, Asylum Casework. Your man isn't who he says he is, and neither, I'm afraid, is she.

CLOSE ON Okonkwo. Her face doesn't move. Her hand goes to the briefcase.

OKONKWO
(very quietly)
I think we should all take a breath.

The girl wakes. Looks at the officers. Looks at Okonkwo.

LENA
Mummy?

Grieve looks at Okonkwo. Then he looks at the barrier arm, and he drops the clipboard.

BACK TO SCENE.
